        ":question/id": "a15a3bf2-ccf2-5108-b6fe-372cf79c1d17",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-31163",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(11-2x)-6{x}^{3}.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(11-2x)$ and $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x)+h(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we differentiate $g(x).$ We express $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(11-2x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u,\\,u=11-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(11-2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-18{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)={g}^{\u2032}(x)+{h}^{\u2032}(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(11-2x)-18{x}^{2}.\n$$"
      },
